# shir_nodes/read_line.node — ONE line from standard input.
#
# Normalises `read VAR` (docs/shir-reductions.md §Normalisation
# transforms): the value is the line WITHOUT its trailing newline; EOF
# yields the empty string. No IFS splitting (the subset's scripts read a
# single variable). Backends: JS runtime sh2.readLine, Perl scalar(<STDIN>)
# chomped, Java BufferedReader stdin, Zig stdin reader.

node ReadLine
tag "ReadLine"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
# Spec: TextOps#read_line_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "ReadLine, ElementRead, FieldRead, TypeAssert: readback laws"). The value is the LF
# model's first feature: the text cut at its first LF with that LF DROPPED. Proved: the
# value contains no LF at all (the chomp law a caller relies on when it compares or
# re-terminates the line); a subject with no LF is read whole and unchanged (the EOF
# case, nothing was terminated); and a subject with an LF splits into the value, that
# terminator, and a remainder.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): the DEVICE — blocking, partial reads, a closed
# descriptor, the file pointer's position, or a final unterminated line being reported
# as EOF belong to the runtime's stream, not this function; encoding; and multi-variable
# `read` with IFS splitting, which is a different lowering.
spec: TextOps#read_line_sound
